= Taming the Bicycle / 驯服自行车

== I

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("I thought the matter over, and concluded I could do it. So I went down and bought a barrel of Pond's Extract and a bicycle. The Expert came home with me to instruct me. We chose the back yard, for the sake of privacy, and went to work.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("我把这件事仔细想了一遍，认定自己能行。于是，我去买了一桶旁氏药液和一辆自行车。专家跟我回家，负责教我。为了不让人看见，我们选了后院，开始练习。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("Mine was not a full-grown bicycle, but only a colt--a fifty-inch, with the pedals shortened up to forty-eight--and skittish, like any other colt. The Expert explained the thing's points briefly, then he got on its back and rode around a little, to show me how easy it was to do. He said that the dismounting was perhaps the hardest thing to learn, and so we would leave that to the last. But he was in error there. He found, to his surprise and joy, that all that he needed to do was to get me on to the machine and stand out of the way; I could get off, myself. Although I was wholly inexperienced, I dismounted in the best time on record. He was on that side, shoving up the machine; we all came down with a crash, he at the bottom, I next, and the machine on top.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("我的自行车还不是一匹成年马，只是一匹小马驹——车轮五十英寸高，脚踏的高度缩到四十八英寸——而且跟别的小马驹一样容易受惊。专家简单讲了讲它的要领，然后骑上它的背，绕了一小圈，让我看看这事有多容易。他说，下车也许是最难学的，所以要留到最后。但在这一点上，他错了。他惊喜地发现，自己只要让我上车，然后闪到一旁，我就能自己下来。尽管毫无经验，我下车的速度却创下了纪录。他在那一边，正扶着车往上推；我们砰的一声全倒了下去，他在最底下，我在中间，车在上面。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("We examined the machine, but it was not in the least injured. This was hardly believable. Yet the Expert assured me that it was true; in fact, the examination proved it. I was partly to realize, then, how admirably these things are constructed. We applied some Pond's Extract, and resumed. The Expert got on the OTHER side to shove up this time, but I dismounted on that side; so the result was as before.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("我们检查了车，竟丝毫无损，实在难以相信。但专家保证这是真的；检查也确实证明如此。我这才开始懂得，这种东西的构造有多么精妙。我们涂上些旁氏药液，继续练。这回专家换到另一边扶车，我却也从那一边下来，所以结果和先前一样。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("The machine was not hurt. We oiled ourselves again, and resumed. This time the Expert took up a sheltered position behind, but somehow or other we landed on him again.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("车没受伤。我们又给自己抹了药，继续。这一次，专家躲在后面一个有掩护的位置，可不知怎么，我们又落到了他身上。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("He was full of admiration; said it was abnormal. She was all right, not a scratch on her, not a timber started anywhere. I said it was wonderful, while we were greasing up, but he said that when I came to know these steel spider-webs I would realize that nothing but dynamite could cripple them. Then he limped out to position, and we resumed once more. This time the Expert took up the position of short-stop, and got a man to shove up behind. We got up a handsome speed, and presently traversed a brick, and I went out over the top of the tiller and landed, head down, on the instructor's back, and saw the machine fluttering in the air between me and the sun. It was well it came down on us, for that broke the fall, and it was not injured.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("他赞叹不已，说简直不同寻常。车好端端的，连道划痕也没有，没一根构件松动。我们给自己涂药时，我说这真了不起；他说，等我了解这些钢铁蜘蛛网，就会知道除了炸药，没什么能弄坏它们。他一瘸一拐地走到位置上，我们再次开始。这回专家站在游击手的位置，又找了一个人在后面推。我们速度颇快，不久轧过一块砖，我从车舵上方飞了出去，头朝下落到教练背上，同时看见那辆车在我与太阳之间的空中翻飞。幸亏它落在了我们身上，摔落的冲击得到缓冲，车也就没受伤。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("Five days later I got out and was carried down to the hospital, and found the Expert doing pretty fairly. In a few more days I was quite sound. I attribute this to my prudence in always dismounting on something soft. Some recommend a feather bed, but I think an Expert is better.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("五天后，我能出门了，被人抬去医院，发现专家恢复得还算不错。再过几天，我就完全好了。我认为这应归功于我的谨慎：下车时总落在柔软的东西上。有人推荐羽绒床，但我觉得专家更好。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("The Expert got out at last, brought four assistants with him. It was a good idea. These four held the graceful cobweb upright while I climbed into the saddle; then they formed in column and marched on either side of me while the Expert pushed behind; all hands assisted at the dismount.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("专家终于出院了，带着四个助手回来。这个主意很好。我爬上车座时，四个人扶住那张优美的蜘蛛网，让它直立；然后他们排成纵队，在我两边行进，专家在后面推。下车时，所有人一起帮忙。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("The bicycle had what is called the \"wabbles,\" and had them very badly. In order to keep my position, a good many things were required of me, and in every instance the thing required was against nature. That is to say, that whatever the needed thing might be, my nature, habit, and breeding moved me to attempt it in one way, while some immutable and unsuspected law of physics required that it be done in just the other way. I perceived by this how radically and grotesquely wrong had been the life-long education of my body and members. They were steeped in ignorance; they knew nothing--nothing which it could profit them to know. For instance, if I found myself falling to the right, I put the tiller hard down the other way, by a quite natural impulse, and so violated a law, and kept on going down. The law required the opposite thing--the big wheel must be turned in the direction in which you are falling. It is hard to believe this, when you are told it. And not merely hard to believe it, but impossible; it is opposed to all your notions. And it is just as hard to do it, after you do come to believe it. Believing it, and knowing by the most convincing proof that it is true, does not help it: you can't any more DO it than you could before; you can neither force nor persuade yourself to do it at first. The intellect has to come to the front, now. It has to teach the limbs to discard their old education and adopt the new.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("自行车有一种叫作“摇晃”的毛病，而且摇晃得非常厉害。为了保持在车上的姿势，我得做许多事情，而每一件都违背天性。也就是说，不管需要做什么，我的本性、习惯和教养都驱使我用一种方法尝试，而某条不可改变、又出乎意料的物理定律，却要求我恰好反着做。由此我才明白，身体和四肢这一生受过的教育，竟错得如此根本、如此荒唐。它们浸透了无知；什么也不知道——不知道任何对它们有用的事情。譬如，发现自己向右倒时，我会出于十分自然的冲动，把车舵猛扳向另一边，结果违反了定律，继续摔下去。定律要求的恰好相反：大车轮必须转向你倾倒的那一边。初次听到这一点，实在难以相信；不只是难以相信，简直无法相信，因为它和你所有观念都相反。等你终于相信了，做起来仍一样难。相信它，甚至有最有说服力的证据知道它是真的，也没用：你还是跟以前一样做不到；起初既不能强迫自己，也不能说服自己这样做。这时候，理智得走到前面，教四肢丢掉旧教育，接受新教育。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("The steps of one's progress are distinctly marked. At the end of each lesson he knows he has acquired something, and he also knows what that something is, and likewise that it will stay with him. It is not like studying German, where you mull along, in a groping, uncertain way, for thirty years; and at last, just as you think you've got it, they spring the subjunctive on you, and there you are. No--and I see now, plainly enough, that the great pity about the German language is, that you can't fall off it and hurt yourself. There is nothing like that feature to make you attend strictly to business. But I also see, by what I have learned of bicycling, that the right and only sure way to learn German is by the bicycling method. That is to say, take a grip on one villainy of it at a time, leaving that one half learned.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("进步的每一个阶段都很清楚。每节课结束时，你知道自己学会了一点东西，也知道那是什么，而且知道它会留在身上。这不像学德语，你得摸索着、不确定地琢磨三十年；最后，正当你以为已经掌握了，他们忽然拿出虚拟式，得，你又完了。不——我如今清楚地看出，德语最大的遗憾，就是你不能从它上面摔下来，把自己摔伤。没有什么比这个特点更能让人全神贯注于正事。不过，根据我学骑车的经验，我也看出，学德语唯一正确而可靠的方法，就是用学骑车的方法：一次抓住它的一种恶习，先学会一半。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("When you have reached the point in bicycling where you can balance the machine tolerably fairly and propel it and steer it, then comes your next task--how to mount it. You do it in this way: you hop along behind it on your right foot, resting the other on the mounting-peg, and grasping the tiller with your hands. At the word, you rise on the peg, stiffen your left leg, hang your other one around in the air in a general in indefinite way, lean your stomach against the rear of the saddle, and then fall off, maybe on one side, maybe on the other; but you fall off. You get up and do it again; and once more; and then several times.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("等你学到能相当稳当地保持平衡、让车前进并转向时，下一项任务就来了——怎样上车。方法是这样的：右脚在车后面跳着走，另一只脚搁在上车踏钉上，两手握住车舵。一声令下，你踏着那枚钉子站起来，绷直左腿，另一条腿不确定地悬在空中乱晃，肚子靠住车座后缘，然后掉下来，可能从这边，也可能从那边；总之你会掉下来。你爬起来，再做一次；再来一次；接着又做几次。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("By this time you have learned to keep your balance; and also to steer without wrenching the tiller out by the roots (I say tiller because it IS a tiller; \"handle-bar\" is a lamely descriptive phrase). So you steer along, straight ahead, a little while, then you rise forward, with a steady strain, bringing your right leg, and then your body, into the saddle, catch your breath, fetch a violent hitch this way and then that, and down you go again.")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("这时，你已经学会保持平衡，也学会转向而不把车舵连根拧下来——我叫它车舵，因为它确实是个舵；“车把”这个说法实在描绘得太无力。于是，你先直直向前骑一会儿，然后身体向前升起，稳稳用力，把右腿、再把身体移到车座上，吸一口气，猛烈地向这边一扭，再向那边一扭，于是又掉下来了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("But you have ceased to mind the going down by this time; you are getting to light on one foot or the other with considerable certainty. Six more attempts and six more falls make you perfect. You land in the saddle comfortably, next time, and stay there--that is, if you can be content to let your legs dangle, and leave the pedals alone a while; but if you grab at once for the pedals, you are gone again. You soon learn to wait a little and perfect your balance before reaching for the pedals; then the mounting-art is acquired, is complete, and a little practice will make it simple and easy to you, though spectators ought to keep off a rod or two to one side, along at first, if you have nothing against them.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("不过，这时你已经不在乎掉下去，落地时一只脚或另一只脚先着地，已经颇有把握。再试六次、再摔六次，你就完善了。下次会舒舒服服落在车座上，而且留在那里——前提是你肯先让双腿垂着，暂时不碰脚踏；若立即去找脚踏，你又完了。很快，你就学会先等一会儿，调整好平衡，再把脚放上去；至此，上车这门艺术便掌握了，完成了。稍加练习，就会觉得简单容易。不过，刚开始时，旁观者最好向一侧让开一两杆远——如果你对他们没有什么意见的话。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("And now you come to the voluntary dismount; you learned the other kind first of all. It is quite easy to tell one how to do the voluntary dismount; the words are few, the requirement simple, and apparently undifficult; let your left pedal go down till your left leg is nearly straight, turn your wheel to the left, and get off as you would from a horse. It certainly does sound exceedingly easy; but it isn't. I don't know why it isn't but it isn't. Try as you may, you don't get down as you would from a horse, you get down as you would from a house afire. You make a spectacle of yourself every time.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("现在轮到主动下车了；另一种下车，你一开始就学过。告诉别人如何主动下车，很容易：话不多，要求简单，看起来也不难。让左脚踏落下，直到左腿差不多伸直，把车轮转向左边，然后像下马一样下来。听起来确实极其容易，但它并不容易。我不知道为什么不容易，可就是不容易。你怎么努力都不像下马，倒像从着火的房子里逃下来。每次都把自己弄成一场笑话。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("During the eight days I took a daily lesson an hour and a half. At the end of this twelve working-hours' appreticeship I was graduated--in the rough. I was pronounced competent to paddle my own bicycle without outside help. It seems incredible, this celerity of acquirement. It takes considerably longer than that to learn horseback-riding in the rough.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("八天里，我每天上一堂一个半小时的课。这十二个工作小时的学徒期结束时，我大致算毕业了，被宣布已经能不用外人帮忙，独自驾驭自己的自行车。这种学习速度简直不可思议；要把骑马学到差不多同样的程度，时间可长得多。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("Now it is true that I could have learned without a teacher, but it would have been risky for me, because of my natural clumsiness. The self-taught man seldom knows anything accurately, and he does not know a tenth as much as he could have known if he had worked under teachers; and, besides, he brags, and is the means of fooling other thoughtless people into going and doing as he himself has done. There are those who imagine that the unlucky accidents of life--life's \"experiences\"--are in some way useful to us. I wish I could find out how. I never knew one of them to happen twice. They always change off and swap around and catch you on your inexperienced side. If personal experience can be worth anything as an education, it wouldn't seem likely that you could trip Methuselah; and yet if that old person could come back here it is more that likely that one of the first things he would do would be to take hold of one of these electric wires and tie himself all up in a knot. Now the surer thing and the wiser thing would be for him to ask somebody whether it was a good thing to take hold of. But that would not suit him; he would be one of the self-taught kind that go by experience; he would want to examine for himself. And he would find, for his instruction, that the coiled patriarch shuns the electric wire; and it would be useful to him, too, and would leave his education in quite a complete and rounded-out condition, till he should come again, some day, and go to bouncing a dynamite-can around to find out what was in it.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("当然，不找老师，我也能学会；但我天生笨拙，那会十分危险。自学的人很少能准确知道什么；他的知识还不到跟着老师学习时可能获得的十分之一。何况，他还爱吹嘘，诱使别的没头脑的人，也照自己的办法去做。有些人以为，生活中那些不幸的事故——所谓生活的“经验”——总会对我们有些用处。我真想知道用处在哪里。我从来没见过同一种事故发生两次；它们总是变换花样，专挑你没经验的一面袭来。如果亲身经验真有教育价值，你似乎很难把玛土撒拉绊倒；但那个老头要是回到这里，他最先做的事情之一，很可能就是抓住一根电线，把自己电得蜷成一个结。更稳妥、更明智的做法，本该是问问别人，这东西值不值得抓。可那不会合他的心意。他会是那种靠经验自学的人，非要亲自检验不可。他会由此获得一条教训：蜷成团的老祖宗应该避开电线。这教训也确实有用，能让他的教育相当完整圆满——直到有一天他又回来，为了弄清炸药罐里装着什么，把罐子拿来颠着玩。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("But we wander from the point. However, get a teacher; it saves much time and Pond's Extract.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("不过，我们扯远了。总之，请找个老师；这样能省下不少时间和旁氏药液。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("Before taking final leave of me, my instructor inquired concerning my physical strength, and I was able to inform him that I hadn't any. He said that that was a defect which would make up-hill wheeling pretty difficult for me at first; but he also said the bicycle would soon remove it. The contrast between his muscles and mine was quite marked. He wanted to test mine, so I offered my biceps--which was my best. It almost made him smile. He said, \"It is pulpy, and soft, and yielding, and rounded; it evades pressure, and glides from under the fingers; in the dark a body might think it was an oyster in a rag.\" Perhaps this made me look grieved, for he added, briskly: \"Oh, that's all right, you needn't worry about that; in a little while you can't tell it from a petrified kidney. Just go right along with your practice; you're all right.\"")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("最后告别以前，教练问起我的体力。我能告诉他的是：我没什么体力。他说，这是一项缺陷，起初会使上坡骑行很困难；不过他也说，自行车很快就能消除这项缺陷。他和我的肌肉差别很明显。他想试试我的，于是我献出了肱二头肌——那是我最好的肌肉。这几乎把他逗笑了。他说：“软绵绵的，柔软，容易变形，圆圆的；一按就躲，从手指下面滑走。在黑暗里，别人可能以为是块破布包着一只牡蛎。”这话也许让我的神色有点伤心，因为他马上轻快地补充：“哦，没关系，不必为这个担心。过不了多久，你就分不清它和一颗石化的肾脏了。只管继续练，你会好的。”")]

== II

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("Then he left me, and I started out alone to seek adventures. You don't really have to seek them--that is nothing but a phrase--they come to you.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("随后他离开了，我开始独自出去寻找冒险。其实不必寻找——那不过是种说法——冒险自己会来找你。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("I chose a reposeful Sabbath-day sort of a back street which was about thirty yards wide between the curbstones. I knew it was not wide enough; still, I thought that by keeping strict watch and wasting no space unnecessarily I could crowd through.")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("我选了一条有安息日般宁静气氛的后街，两边路缘石之间大约三十码宽。我知道这还不够宽；不过我想，只要严加注意、不浪费空间，也许还挤得过去。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("Of course I had trouble mounting the machine, entirely on my own responsibility, with no encouraging moral support from the outside, no sympathetic instructor to say, \"Good! now you're doing well--good again--don't hurry--there, now, you're all right--brace up, go ahead.\" In place of this I had some other support. This was a boy, who was perched on a gate-post munching a hunk of maple sugar.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("当然，全由自己负责上车，我遇到了困难。没有外来的鼓励作为精神支柱，没有富于同情心的教练说：“很好！现在你做得很好——又做对了——别着急——好了，你现在行了——振作起来，继续。”取而代之，我得到的是另一种支持：一个坐在门柱上、嚼着一大块枫糖的男孩。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("He was full of interest and comment. The first time I failed and went down he said that if he was me he would dress up in pillows, that's what he would do. The next time I went down he advised me to go and learn to ride a tricycle first. The third time I collapsed he said he didn't believe I could stay on a horse-car. But the next time I succeeded, and got clumsily under way in a weaving, tottering, uncertain fashion, and occupying pretty much all of the street. My slow and lumbering gait filled the boy to the chin with scorn, and he sung out, \"My, but don't he rip along!\" Then he got down from his post and loafed along the sidewalk, still observing and occasionally commenting. Presently he dropped into my wake and followed along behind. A little girl passed by, balancing a wash-board on her head, and giggled, and seemed about to make a remark, but the boy said, rebukingly, \"Let him alone, he's going to a funeral.\"")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("他兴致勃勃，也很爱评论。我第一次失败、摔下来时，他说，换作自己就会穿上一身枕头，他准这么办。下一次摔下去，他建议我先学骑三轮车。第三次，他说不信我连一辆马车都坐得住。但下次我终于成功了，笨拙地开始前进，迂回、摇摆、迟疑，几乎占据了整条街道。那缓慢而笨重的步态，使男孩的轻蔑从一边下巴漫到了另一边；他喊道：“哎呀，他可真是在飞奔哪！”随后，他从门柱上下来，沿人行道慢悠悠地走，一边看，一边不时发表评论。不久，他跟到了我后面，尾随着我。一个小女孩头顶搓衣板走过，咯咯笑着，像要说话；男孩却训她：“别理他，他赶着去参加葬礼呢。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("I have been familiar with that street for years, and had always supposed it was a dead level; but it was not, as the bicycle now informed me, to my surprise. The bicycle, in the hands of a novice, is as alert and acute as a spirit-level in the detecting the delicate and vanishing shades of difference in these matters. It notices a rise where your untrained eye would not observe that one existed; it notices any decline which water will run down. I was toiling up a slight rise, but was not aware of it. It made me tug and pant and perspire; and still, labor as I might, the machine came almost to a standstill every little while. At such times the boy would say: \"That's it! take a rest--there ain't no hurry. They can't hold the funeral without YOU.\"")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("那条街我熟悉多年，一直以为它绝对平坦；如今自行车却告诉我并非如此，令我十分吃惊。自行车到了新手手里，就像水平仪一样敏锐，连那些极细微、几乎消失的高低差都能察觉。未经训练的眼睛看不出的上坡，它能发现；凡能让水流下来的下坡，它也能发现。我正费力爬一段微坡，自己却不知道。它使我用力、气喘、出汗；尽管使尽劲，那辆车还是每隔一会儿就几乎停住。这时候，男孩便说：“对啦！歇一歇——不着急。没有你，他们也开不了葬礼。”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("Stones were a bother to me. Even the smallest ones gave me a panic when I went over them. I could hit any kind of a stone, no matter how small, if I tried to miss it; and of course at first I couldn't help trying to do that. It is but natural. It is part of the ass that is put in us all, for some inscrutable reason.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("石头使我烦恼。即使最小的石子，轧过去也会让我惊慌。只要想避开，无论石头多小，我都能撞上；当然，起初我又忍不住想避开。这很自然。由于某种不可解释的原因，我们每个人身上都被放进了一点蠢驴的成分。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("It was at the end of my course, at last, and it was necessary for me to round to. This is not a pleasant thing, when you undertake it for the first time on your own responsibility, and neither is it likely to succeed. Your confidence oozes away, you fill steadily up with nameless apprehensions, every fiber of you is tense with a watchful strain, you start a cautious and gradual curve, but your squirmy nerves are all full of electric anxieties, so the curve is quickly demoralized into a jerky and perilous zigzag; then suddenly the nickel-clad horse takes the bit in its mouth and goes slanting for the curbstone, defying all prayers and all your powers to change its mind--your heart stands still, your breath hangs fire, your legs forget to work, straight on you go, and there are but a couple of feet between you and the curb now. And now is the desperate moment, the last chance to save yourself; of course all your instructions fly out of your head, and you whirl your wheel AWAY from the curb instead of TOWARD it, and so you go sprawling on that granite-bound inhospitable shore. That was my luck; that was my experience. I dragged myself out from under the indestructible bicycle and sat down on the curb to examine.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("终于到了我这趟路的尽头，必须掉头。第一次独自负责做这件事，并不愉快，也不容易成功。你的信心一点点漏掉，难以名状的恐惧却不断灌进来，全身每根纤维都警觉地绷紧。你小心地开始画一道平缓的弧线，但那些扭动的神经都充满触电般的焦虑，弧线很快失去秩序，变成急促而危险的锯齿。突然，那匹镀镍的马咬住了嚼子，斜奔路缘石，无视所有祈祷，也不理你改变它心意的一切努力。你的心停住，呼吸卡住，双腿忘了动作，你直冲过去，现在离路缘只剩几英尺。这是绝望的时刻，最后一次自救机会；当然，全部教导都从脑海里飞走了，你不是把车轮转向路缘，反而转离它，于是摊倒在那道以花岗岩为边、毫不友好的岸上。这就是我的运气，我的经验。我从那辆摔不坏的自行车下面拖出自己，坐在路缘上检查。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("I started on the return trip. It was now that I saw a farmer's wagon poking along down toward me, loaded with cabbages. If I needed anything to perfect the precariousness of my steering, it was just that. The farmer was occupying the middle of the road with his wagon, leaving barely fourteen or fifteen yards of space on either side. I couldn't shout at him--a beginner can't shout; if he opens his mouth he is gone; he must keep all his attention on his business. But in this grisly emergency, the boy came to the rescue, and for once I had to be grateful to him. He kept a sharp lookout on the swiftly varying impulses and inspirations of my bicycle, and shouted to the man accordingly:")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("我开始往回骑。这时，看见一个农夫驾着满载卷心菜的马车，慢吞吞地向我驶来。如果还需要什么来使我本已不稳定的转向达到完美，就是它了。农夫的马车占着路中央，两边各只留下十四五码空间。我不能向他喊——初学者不能喊；一张嘴，他就完了，必须把全部注意力放在手头的事上。但在这可怕的紧急关头，男孩来救场了；这一次，我不得不感激他。他密切观察自行车那迅速变换的冲动与灵感，随时向农夫喊话：")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("\"To the left! Turn to the left, or this jackass 'll run over you!\" The man started to do it. \"No, to the right, to the right! Hold on! THAT won't do!--to the left!--to the right!--to the LEFT--right! left--ri-- Stay where you ARE, or you're a goner!\"")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("“往左！往左转，不然这个蠢驴就要轧到你了！”那人开始照办。“不，往右，往右！停住！那样不行！——往左！——往右！——往左——右！左——右——待在原地，不然你就完了！”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("And just then I caught the off horse in the starboard and went down in a pile. I said, \"Hang it! Couldn't you SEE I was coming?\"")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("就在这时，我撞到了右边那匹马的右舷，倒成一堆。我说：“该死！你难道没看见我要过来吗？”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("\"Yes, I see you was coming, but I couldn't tell which WAY you was coming. Nobody could--now, COULD they? You couldn't yourself--now, COULD you? So what could _I_ do?\"")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("“看见了，我看见你要过来，可不知道你打算从哪边来。谁能知道呢——你说，能吗？连你自己也不知道——你说，对吗？那我能怎么办？”")]

#html.p(id: "en-S029")[#text("There was something in that, and so I had the magnanimity to say so. I said I was no doubt as much to blame as he was.")]

#zh(id: "S029")[#text("这话有点道理，我便宽宏大量地承认了。我说，毫无疑问，我和他同样有错。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S030")[#text("Within the next five days I achieved so much progress that the boy couldn't keep up with me. He had to go back to his gate-post, and content himself with watching me fall at long range.")]

#zh(id: "S030")[#text("接下来五天里，我进步这么大，那个男孩已经跟不上了。他只好回到门柱上，满足于远远看着我摔跤。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S031")[#text("There was a row of low stepping-stones across one end of the street, a measured yard apart. Even after I got so I could steer pretty fairly I was so afraid of those stones that I always hit them. They gave me the worst falls I ever got in that street, except those which I got from dogs. I have seen it stated that no expert is quick enough to run over a dog; that a dog is always able to skip out of his way. I think that that may be true: but I think that the reason he couldn't run over the dog was because he was trying to. I did not try to run over any dog. But I ran over every dog that came along. I think it makes a great deal of difference. If you try to run over the dog he knows how to calculate, but if you are trying to miss him he does not know how to calculate, and is liable to jump the wrong way every time. It was always so in my experience. Even when I could not hit a wagon I could hit a dog that came to see me practice. They all liked to see me practice, and they all came, for there was very little going on in our neighborhood to entertain a dog. It took time to learn to miss a dog, but I achieved even that.")]

#zh(id: "S031")[#text("街道一端横着一排低矮的踏脚石，间隔恰好一码。即使我已经能转向得相当不错，仍怕那些石头，结果总撞上。在那条街上，除了因狗而摔的几跤，最惨的就是它们给我的。我看见有人说，没有哪个骑车专家能快到轧中一只狗；狗总能跳开。我觉得也许如此，但我认为，他轧不中狗，是因为他想轧它。我没想轧任何狗，却轧过每一只经过的狗。我认为区别很大。你想轧它，它就知道怎样算；你想避开它，它反而不知道怎样算，每次都很可能跳错方向。按我的经验，事情一直如此。即使撞不到一辆马车，我也能撞到一只来看我练习的狗。它们都爱看我练习，全都来了，因为我们这一带，能供狗消遣的事很少。学会避开一只狗花了些时间，但连这点，我最终也做到了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S032")[#text("I can steer as well as I want to, now, and I will catch that boy one of these days and run over HIM if he doesn't reform.")]

#zh(id: "S032")[#text("现在，我想怎么转就能怎么转了。有一天我会逮住那个男孩；他要是还不改过自新，我就从他身上轧过去。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S033")[#text("Get a bicycle. You will not regret it, if you live.")]

#zh(id: "S033")[#text("买一辆自行车吧。只要你还活着，就不会后悔。")]
